  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 101 PM MDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery shows increasing cloud cover across\neastern Idaho as southwesterly flow strengthens ahead of an \napproaching upper-level trough. Temperatures have climbed into \nthe 60s across lower elevations and will reach the 70s before \npeak diurnal heating concludes. As the trough axis approaches \ntonight, cloud cover will thicken, bringing a brief return of \nshowers and potential isolated thunderstorms to the eastern half\nof the forecast area. The highest precipitation probabilities \nwill align along and east of the I-15 corridor, with the Eastern\nHighlands serving as the primary focus area. This feature will \nmove through quickly, with clearing conditions spreading across \nthe region throughout the daytime hours on Monday. The system \nwill usher in a cooler air mass, dropping valley highs back into\nthe 60s on Monday\u2014approximately 10 degrees below today's \nmaximums.\n\nUpper-level flow transitions to predominantly zonal Tuesday \ninto Wednesday as high pressure begins to organize over the \nSouthwestern U.S.. Temperatures will hover near seasonal \nclimatological norms for the first part of the workweek, \naccompanied by dry conditions and light surface winds. Upper- \nlevel ridging will build directly over the Intermountain West \nstarting Wednesday and remain established through next weekend. \nThis will drive a warming trend that will push afternoon \ntemperatures above seasonal averages. Highs in lower-elevation \nvalleys will reach the mid-to-upper 70s by Thursday and hold \nthere through the weekend. With atmospheric moisture shunted \nwell away from the region, the forecast remains completely void \nof precipitation chances beyond Monday, ensuring a dry, \npleasant start to October.",
